ian Repertoire",{"r1bqkbnr/pp1pnppp/8/3P4/8/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 1 7":21,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2nP4/8/8/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 1 8":22,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/3Pn3/8/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 1 7":23,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/3P4/1n6/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 1 7":24,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/3P4/8/5n2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 8":25,"r1bqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/5n2/3P4/8/5Q2/PP3PPP/RNB1KB1R w KQkq - 1 9":26,"r1bqkbnr/pp3ppp/3p4/3P4/Qn6/2N5/PP3PPP/R1B1KBNR b KQkq - 1 8":27,"r1b1kbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/q2P4/1n6/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 3 8":28,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2n1p3/8/3PP3/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 5":29,"r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/8/3pP3/2P5/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 4":30,"r1bqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/5n2/3P4/1n6/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 3 8":31,"r1b1kb1r/pp1p1ppp/5n2/q2P4/8/3B1Q2/PP3PPP/RNB1K2R w KQkq - 3 10":32,"r1b1k2r/pp1p1ppp/5n2/q2P4/1b6/2NBQ3/PP3PPP/R1B1K2R b KQkq - 6 11":33,"r1bk1b1r/pp1p1ppp/5n2/q2PN3/1n6/2N5/PP2QPPP/R1B1KB1R b KQ - 8 10":34,"r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/2p5/4P3/2P5/PP1P1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 1 3":35,"r1b1k2r/pp1p1ppp/5n2/q2P4/1b6/2NB1Q2/PP3PPP/R1B1K2R w KQkq - 5 11":36,"r1b1kb1r/pp1p1ppp/1q1P1n2/8/1n3B2/2N5/PP3PPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 2 9":37,"r1b1kb1r/pp1p1ppp/5n2/q2P4/1n6/2N2N2/PP3PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 5 9":38},"Oops, that was a blunder. Play d6","Now check with Qe2, which will win a piece","This is black's most common reply, and we already experience an advantage here because of our central control. We play the move Nf3 offering a trade","Another mistake! Great. Nc3 first to defend the pawn. This is an important move, because it will also defend against Qa5 checks in the future","Now we recapture","Our plan here is to quickly castle and develop. If black ever moves their queen from the defence of the e7 square, we have a wonderful tactic!","That's a free piece. Easy money","Why can't we just push to d6 with the same idea? Well, black has this attacking resource with Qe5+. To stop this, we play a normal developing move Nf3","Here, we take more space in the center with d5","85% of the time black plays that move. Take back. ","Also a blunder. Play d6","Yes! This is great news. Block with Nc3","In this position, black can choose to trap their own bishop by blocking the check, or to move their king in a very dynamic and open position","How can black defend against this? ","You should be pretty happy when you see this move, because it is actually already a mistake. Black should've tried to attack your e4 pawn. Play d4","This is the most common move, and it results in an easy tactic with Qe3+","Black has no way to castle here, and no way to develop, while your moves should be very straightforward. ","Now that we have prevented the Qe5+, we can continue with either d6 OR Qe2+. Qe2+ is a bit better, because we are going to take away black's castling rights and follow-up with Ne5",{"r1bqkbnr/pp1pnppp/3P4/8/8/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq -":40,"r1bqkbnr/pp1pnppp/8/3P4/8/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -":44,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2nP4/8/8/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -":47,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/3Pn3/8/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -":50,"r1bqk1nr/pp1pbppp/2nP4/8/8/8/PP2QPPP/RNB1KBNR w KQkq -":53,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2n5/3P4/8/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq -":56,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2nP4/8/8/8/PP2QPPP/RNB1KBNR b KQkq -":59,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/3P4/1n6/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -":62,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/3P4/8/5Q2/PP3PPP/RNB1KB1R b KQkq -":65,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/3P4/8/5n2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq -":67,"rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq -":68,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/3Pn3/8/5N2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq -":71,"r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/8/3PP3/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq -":73,"r1bqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/5n2/3P4/8/5Q2/PP3PPP/RNB1KB1R w KQkq -":75,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2n5/3p4/4P3/8/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -":77,"r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/3P4/1n6/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq -":79,"r1bqkbnr/pp3ppp/3p4/3P4/1n6/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq -":81,"rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -":83,"r1b1kbnr/pp1p1ppp/8/q2P4/1n6/2N5/PP3PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq -":86,"r1bqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/5n2/3P4/8/3B